# Limit battery charge to 40-60% to minimise wear on an always-on machine.
#
# The T480 sits plugged in 24/7 in the AV cabinet; the battery's only job
# is to bridge transient power blips, not to power the laptop on the go.
# Lithium cells last dramatically longer if kept in the middle of their
# SoC range rather than floated at 100%. See README's "Power management"
# section for the full rationale.
#
# We use a udev rule rather than tmpfiles.d because charge_control_*
# are power_supply subsystem attributes, and udev handles the event
# timing naturally (fires when each battery enumerates, fires again on
# resume if the EC reset thresholds, no-ops cleanly if a battery is
# absent). tmpfiles.d would run once at boot regardless of battery
# presence and log an error if BAT1 was missing.
#
# Write order matters: kernel rejects start > current_end and end <
# current_start. Setting start first (40, always <= current end of 100)
# then end (60, now >= new start of 40) keeps both writes valid.
ACTION=="add|change", SUBSYSTEM=="power_supply", ATTR{type}=="Battery", \
  ATTR{charge_control_start_threshold}="40", \
  ATTR{charge_control_end_threshold}="60"
